In Java, a static variable is a variable that belongs to the class rather than to instances of the class. It is shared by all the objects of that class and can be accessed directly using the class name, without creating an instance of the class.

To define a static variable in Java, the `static` keyword is used before the variable declaration. It is usually declared at the class level, outside any method or constructor.

Here's an example to understand the concept and practical usage of static variables:

```java
public class Employee {
    private static int nextId = 1; // static variable

    private int id;
    private String name;

    public Employee(String name) {
        this.name = name;
        this.id = nextId++; // assigning unique id using static variable
    }

    public static int getNextId() {
        return nextId; // static method accessing static variable
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public int getId() {
        return id;
    }
}
```

In the above example, we have a `nextId` static variable which keeps track of the next available ID for an `Employee` object. It is incremented each time a new `Employee` is created.

The constructor `Employee(String name)` initializes the `name` variable and assigns a unique `id` to each object using the static variable `nextId`.

The static method `getNextId()` allows us to access the value of `nextId` from outside the class without creating an instance of `Employee`. It can be useful for getting the next available ID or performing any logic based on the static variable.

By using static variables in this scenario, we ensure that each `Employee` object gets a unique ID without having to rely on external logic or manually managing the ID assignment.

