Maagizo ya preprocessor katika C++ ni maagizo yanayotolewa kwa mkusanyiko kabla ya mchakato halisi wa ukusanyaji kuanza. Yanashughulikiwa na preprocessor, ambayo ni sehemu tofauti ya mkusanyiko. Maagizo haya yanatumika kubadilisha msimbo wa chanzo kabla ya kukusanywa, ikiruhusu waendelezaji kujumuisha au kutenga sehemu maalum za msimbo kulingana na masharti au mipangilio fulani.

Matumizi moja ya kawaida ya maagizo ya preprocessor ni kuzuia kujumuishwa mara nyingi kwa faili za kichwa. Faili za kichwa kwa kawaida zina prototypes za kazi, matangazo ya darasa, na matangazo mengine yanayoshirikiwa kati ya faili kadhaa za chanzo. Wakati faili kadhaa za chanzo zinapojumuisha faili moja ya kichwa, inaweza kusababisha makosa kama vile ufafanuzi wa alama za kurudiwa.

Ili kuzuia hili, C++ inatumia agizo la preprocessor linaloitwa "include guards" au "header guards". Hii inahusisha kufunga maudhui yote ya faili ya kichwa na jozi ya maagizo ya masharti ambayo yanakagua ikiwa alama fulani imewekwa. Ikiwa alama hiyo haijaanzishwa, maudhui ya faili ya kichwa yanajumuishwa na alama hiyo inawekwa. Ikiwa alama hiyo tayari imewekwa, faili ya kichwa inakosolewa.

Hapa kuna mfano unaoonyesha matumizi ya maagizo ya preprocessor katika programu ya C++ yenye faili kadhaa za kichwa:

```cpp
// header1.h
#ifndef HEADER1_H
#define HEADER1_H

void function1();

#endif

// header2.h
#ifndef HEADER2_H
#define HEADER2_H

void function2();

#endif

// main.cpp
#include "header1.h"
#include "header2.h"

int main() {
    function1();
    function2();

    return 0;
}

// implementation.cpp
#include "header1.h"

void function1() {
    // utekelezaji wa kazi
}

// implementation2.cpp
#include "header2.h"

void function2() {
    // utekelezaji wa kazi
}
```

Katika mfano huu, faili za kichwa "header1.h" na "header2.h" zimewekwa na maagizo ya preprocessor. Wakati mkusanyiko unakutana na agizo la kwanza la kujumuisha kwa kila faili ya kichwa, inakagua ikiwa alama inayohusiana (kwa mfano, "HEADER1_H") imewekwa. Ikiwa la, inajumuisha maudhui ya faili ya kichwa na kuweka alama hiyo. Ikiwa alama hiyo tayari imewekwa (kutokana na kujumuishwa kwa awali), faili ya kichwa inakosolewa.

Kwa kutumia include guards, tunahakikisha kuwa kila faili ya kichwa inajumuishwa mara moja tu katika programu, hata kama faili kadhaa za chanzo zinazijumuisha. Hii husaidia kuzuia ufafanuzi wa alama za kurudiwa na kuhakikisha kuwa programu inakusanywa kwa usahihi.

output_en:
Preprocessor directives in C++ are instructions given to the compiler before the actual compilation process begins. They are processed by the preprocessor, which is a separate component of the compiler. These directives are used to modify the source code before it is compiled, allowing developers to include or exclude specific sections of code based on certain conditions or configurations.

One common use of preprocessor directives is to prevent multiple inclusion of header files. Header files typically contain function prototypes, class declarations, and other declarations that are shared across multiple source files. When multiple source files include the same header file, it can lead to errors such as duplicate symbol definitions.

To prevent this, C++ uses a preprocessor directive called "include guards" or "header guards". This involves wrapping the entire contents of a header file with a pair of conditional directives that check if a certain symbol has been defined. If the symbol has not been defined, the header file's contents are included and the symbol is defined. If the symbol has already been defined, the header file is skipped.

Here's an example illustrating the use of preprocessor directives in a C++ program with multiple header files:

```cpp
// header1.h
#ifndef HEADER1_H
#define HEADER1_H

void function1();

#endif

// header2.h
#ifndef HEADER2_H
#define HEADER2_H

void function2();

#endif

// main.cpp
#include "header1.h"
#include "header2.h"

int main() {
    function1();
    function2();

    return 0;
}

// implementation.cpp
#include "header1.h"

void function1() {
    // function implementation
}

// implementation2.cpp
#include "header2.h"

void function2() {
    // function implementation
}
```

In this example, the header files "header1.h" and "header2.h" are guarded with preprocessor directives. When the compiler encounters the first include directive for each header file, it checks if the corresponding symbol (e.g., "HEADER1_H") has been defined. If not, it includes the header file's contents and defines the symbol. If the symbol has already been defined (due to a previous inclusion), the header file is skipped.

By using include guards, we ensure that each header file is included only once in the program, even if multiple source files include them. This helps prevent duplicate symbol definitions and ensures that the program compiles correctly.

