In C++, the 'const' keyword is used to declare a constant. It is used to indicate that a variable, parameter, or member function should not be modified. This keyword plays a crucial role in enforcing immutability and preventing unintended modifications to data.

When used with a member function in a class, the 'const' keyword indicates that the function will not modify any member variables of the class. This helps in ensuring that calling the function will not alter the state of the object.

Here's an example of how the 'const' keyword can be used to create a constant member function in a class:

```cpp
class MyClass {
private:
    int myNumber;
public:
    MyClass(int number) : myNumber(number) {}

    // Constant member function that does not modify any member variables
    int getNumber() const {
        return myNumber;
    }
};
```

In the above example, the 'getNumber()' member function is declared as a constant member function using the 'const' keyword. This means that it guarantees not to modify any member variables of the class.

